WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.200
Look at a fly wing.

2
00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:10.560
Each cell grows one hair, and every hair points toward the wing tip.

3
00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:16.520
A direction shared by the cells in the plane of a sheet is called planar cell

4
00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:21.700
polarity, or PCP. But no cell can see the whole sheet.

5
00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:24.920
So how does each cell know which way to point?

6
00:00:25.933 --> 00:00:33.313
At the University of Virginia, in 1987, Charles Vinson and Paul Adler made patches

7
00:00:33.313 --> 00:00:36.133
of cells that lack a gene called frizzled.

8
00:00:36.893 --> 00:00:38.573
The rest of each wing was normal.

9
00:00:39.553 --> 00:00:42.093
Would the normal cells beside a patch notice?

10
00:00:43.067 --> 00:00:47.747
Normal cells on the tip side of a patch grew misdirected hairs.

11
00:00:48.787 --> 00:00:50.707
Normal cells on the other side did not.

12
00:00:51.587 --> 00:00:57.447
So a patch with no frizzled upset its normal neighbours, but on one side only.

13
00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:02.240
Two years later, Adler's laboratory cloned frizzled.

14
00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:05.420
It encodes a seven-pass membrane protein.

15
00:01:06.220 --> 00:01:10.920
It is the same Frizzled as in the Wnt receptor family, which is named for untidy

16
00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:16.360
hairs on a fly. This pathway does not use β-catenin or TCF.

17
00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:22.560
In the wing, its output is the place where the cell builds the actin bundle that becomes its hair.
